## Core Methodology

The Free Energy Mixer reconceptualizes the memory readout process as a variational free-energy optimization problem. Instead of applying a uniform convex combination across channels, FEM introduces a value-aware, per-channel selection mechanism that operates before the traditional mixing step.

The key insight is to formulate readout as an optimization problem for each channel independently. For channel $j$j, FEM seeks an index distribution $q$q that maximizes expected utility subject to an information budget constraint:

$$\max_q \mathbb{E}_{i \sim q}[v_{i,j}] \quad \text{subject to} \quad \text{KL}(q \| p_t) \leq B_{t,j}$$q max​E i∼q​[v i,j​]subject to KL(q∥p t​)≤B t,j​

where $p_t$p t​ represents the prior distribution (derived from query-key similarities in attention) and $B_{t,j}$B t,j​ is the information budget for channel $j$j at time $t$t.

Using Lagrange multipliers with inverse temperature $\beta_{t,j}$β t,j​, this optimization yields the free energy output:
